{
  "_": "Factorio id -> Minecraft item. Hand-maintained; see docs/MAPPING.md.",
  "_generated_from": "reference/factorio/data-raw-2.0.77.json, Factorio 2.0.77 base game (tools/factorio_data.py)",
  "_size": "Factorio tile footprint [width, depth], for entities larger than one tile. Absent means 1x1. One Minecraft block per tile - see docs/NEXT.md.",
  "_speed": "Factorio belt speed in tiles per second. Identity: it decides what a factory looks like, and throughput follows from it - four items a tile a lane, two lanes, so 1.875 tiles/s is the wiki's 15 items/s. tools/check_models.py holds each belt block's SPEED constant to this.",
  "_stack": "Factorio's stack size: a fact a player carries in their head, so it is written here and tools/check_models.py holds the code to it. An item the pack registers says it through nauvis_lib's Stacks.of(n); a vanilla stand-in gets it from the pack mod's StandInStacks. Minecraft's own ceiling of 99 is lifted by nauvis_lib's mixins, so the number here is the number in a chest. Entered by hand, like size; data.raw's stack_size is the source.",
  "_recipes": "A recipe that makes the item it is named after needs only that item's entry. Any other recipe - the refinery's, cracking, the solid-fuel routes, emptying a barrel - is filed under its own name and needs a row here: an owner, or a skip and a reason. `launch` names the cargo of a launch that data.raw has no recipe for, and `show` generates a recipe data.raw hides. tools/factorio_data.py refuses a new recipe with neither an item entry nor a row, so a new dump cannot drop one silently.",
